b630506af4cac17271d0f38ad465419b033a807e6dbc" "q-340456/selection/396e089cc88788b4f031a4d7b34af22d8bf30be364fa3074c948e483ea863fff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340456" :question/id #uuid "c190d1a3-d3a0-5456-a806-230d7c8a4df3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340456" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x-7x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x+7x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(19x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(19x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+\\frac{1}{38}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(19x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340456/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/